window.PG_TRAILS = {"trails":[{"id":"women","url":"trails/women.html","title":"Women of Cape Ann","kicker":"by car, with a walk on Rocky Neck","span":"1850s to 1980","short":"A full day by car","lead":"Ten stops for the women who painted Gloucester, from Fitz Henry Lane’s circle in the 1850s to Nell Blaine’s harbor in the 1950s. Most stops are outdoors and free; the museum stop is the one place to see several of their paintings at once.","legs":[[0,3,"drive"],[3,7,"walk"],[7,10,"drive"]],"hero":{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","alt":"Mary Blood Mellen, Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets), about 1870"},"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Stage Fort Park","who":"Mary Blood Mellen (1819–1886)","addr":"Hough Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.6057,-70.67845],"approx":false,"text":"A minister’s wife from Sterling, Massachusetts, Mellen came into Fitz Henry Lane’s world after her brother-in-law took the Universalist pulpit in Gloucester in 1855. By the middle of the decade she was working beside Lane, with free use of his drawings and at times his canvases…","works":[{"id":"mellen-field-beach","t":"Field Beach, Stage Fort Park","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"c. 1850","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"},{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"about 1870","img":true,"mus":"Bowdoin College Museum of Art, Brunswick, Maine"}]},{"n":2,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"The collection","addr":"27 Pleasant Street, Gloucester","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"The museum owns Theresa Bernstein’s <i>Gloucester in Blue</i> (1925), Nell Blaine’s <i>Wharf Studio, Gloucester</i> (1958) and the archive of the Folly Cove Designers; the Rocky Neck Art Colony also credits it with Martha Walter’s <i>Rocky Neck Store and My Studio</i> (1920). Ask at the desk which are on view.","works":[{"id":"bernstein-gloucester-in-blue","t":"Gloucester in Blue","by":"Theresa Bernstein","d":"1925","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"},{"id":"blaine-wharf-studio","t":"Wharf Studio, Gloucester","by":"Nell Blaine","d":"1958","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"}]},{"n":3,"name":"Our Lady of Good Voyage","who":"Josephine Nivison (1883–1968)","addr":"142 Prospect Street, Gloucester","ll":[42.61718,-70.65715],"approx":false,"text":"In the summer of 1923 Jo Nivison painted <i>Church Towers, Gloucester</i>, a head-on view of this church’s twin towers. It was the summer she and Edward Hopper painted together here, and she encouraged him to take up watercolor. We have not found the painting in a public collection.","works":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Rocky Neck Guest House","who":"Theresa Bernstein (1890–2002)","addr":"1 Eastern Point Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.59873,-70.6548],"approx":false,"text":"Bernstein first came to Gloucester in 1916. She and the painter William Meyerowitz stayed here after their marriage in 1919, and in 1924 bought a house on Mount Pleasant Avenue in East Gloucester (a private home), where they hosted friends including Milton Avery. Both helped found the North Shore Arts Association.","works":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Martha Walter’s studio","who":"Martha Walter (1875–1976)","addr":"37 Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.60581,-70.65739],"approx":false,"text":"A Philadelphia painter trained under William Merritt Chase, Walter had joined the Gloucester colony by 1913 and kept a studio and gallery on Rocky Neck from the later 1910s into the 1920s. The building is now a gallery.","works":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Sally Michel’s studio","who":"Sally Michel Avery (1902–2003)","addr":"77–78 Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.60757,-70.6559],"approx":false,"text":"In 1924 Sally Michel rented the studio next to Milton Avery’s here. They married in 1926, and her work supported the family while he painted.","works":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Rocky Neck wharves","who":"Nell Blaine (1922–1996)","addr":"Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.605,-70.657],"approx":true,"text":"Blaine rented studios on Rocky Neck in 1954 and 1958; the Whitney’s <i>Harbor and Green Cloth, II</i> dates from the second summer. From 1974 until her death she summered in East Gloucester. The marker is placed on the avenue; the studio addresses are not published.","works":[{"id":"blaine-harbor-green-cloth","t":"Harbor and Green Cloth, II","by":"Nell Blaine","d":"1958","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":8,"name":"Bass Rocks","who":"Martha Walter","addr":"Atlantic Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.61426,-70.63532],"approx":false,"text":"Walter’s entries at the Pennsylvania Academy in 1915 included a crowded beach at Bass Rocks; Philadelphia critics praised the Gloucester picture. Parking along Atlantic Road is limited.","works":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Good Harbor Beach","who":"Theresa Bernstein","addr":"Thatcher Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":false,"text":"Bernstein was still painting this beach around 1980, when she was about ninety: the Brooklyn Museum owns <i>Rabbits, Good Harbor Beach, Gloucester</i>. Summer parking is by fee.","works":[{"id":"bernstein-rabbits","t":"Rabbits, Good Harbor Beach, Gloucester","by":"Theresa Bernstein","d":"about 1980","img":false,"mus":"Brooklyn Museum, New York"}]},{"n":10,"name":"Folly Cove, Lanesville","who":"Virginia Lee Burton (1909–1968)","addr":"Washington Street (Route 127), Lanesville","ll":[42.68839,-70.64125],"approx":false,"text":"The children’s author of <i>Mike Mulligan and His Steam Shovel</i> taught block printing to her neighbors here and in 1941 founded the Folly Cove Designers, who printed textiles by hand until 1969. Their blocks, fabrics and records are at the Cape Ann Museum.","works":[]}]},{"id":"hopper","url":"trails/hopper.html","title":"Edward Hopper’s Gloucester","kicker":"a downtown walk, then a drive","span":"1912 to 1934","short":"About four hours, walk and drive","lead":"Hopper came to Gloucester in 1912 with his friend Leon Kroll, and again in the summers of 1923, 1924, 1926 and 1928, mostly painting houses in watercolor. Twelve of his subjects can still be found.","legs":[[0,8,"walk"],[8,12,"drive"]],"hero":null,"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"Start","addr":"27 Pleasant Street","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"Start here: the museum has the most Hopper context in town, and its docent walks cover the downtown houses.","works":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Universalist Church","who":"<i>Universalist Church</i>, 1926","addr":"10 Church Street","ll":[42.6133,-70.66623],"approx":false,"text":"The church on Church Street. The watercolor is at the Princeton University Art Museum.","works":[{"id":"hopper-universalist","t":"Universalist Church","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1926","img":false,"mus":"Princeton University Art Museum"}]},{"n":3,"name":"Anderson’s House","who":"<i>Anderson’s House</i>","addr":"18 Western Avenue","ll":[42.61065,-70.66692],"approx":false,"text":"A tall yellow Italianate house just up Western Avenue from Stacy Boulevard. The watercolor is at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.","works":[{"id":"hopper-andersons","t":"Anderson's House","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1926 per museum (inscribed “Gloucester 1923”)","img":false,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}]},{"n":4,"name":"The Fort","who":"<i>Italian Quarter, Gloucester</i>, 1912","addr":"Fort Square, off Commercial Street","ll":[42.60832,-70.66457],"approx":false,"text":"On his first visit, in 1912, Hopper painted the fishermen’s houses of the Fort, Gloucester’s Italian neighborhood.","works":[{"id":"hopper-italian-quarter","t":"Italian Quarter, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":5,"name":"Haskell’s House","who":"<i>Haskell’s House</i>, 1924","addr":"316 Main Street, at Prospect Street","ll":[42.61595,-70.65585],"approx":false,"text":"The Second Empire house at the corner of Main and Prospect Streets. The watercolor is at the National Gallery of Art.","works":[{"id":"hopper-haskells-house","t":"Haskell's House","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1924","img":false,"mus":"National Gallery of Art, Washington"}]},{"n":6,"name":"Our Lady of Good Voyage","who":"Hopper and Jo Nivison, 1923","addr":"from Mount Vernon Street","ll":[42.61775,-70.6575],"approx":false,"text":"Hopper painted the church’s towers from a distance, from about 15–17 Mount Vernon Street; in the same summer Jo Nivison painted them head-on.","works":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Sun on Prospect Street","who":"<i>Sun on Prospect Street</i>, 1934","addr":"98–102 Prospect Street","ll":[42.61593,-70.66034],"approx":false,"text":"An oil, at the Cincinnati Art Museum.","works":[{"id":"hopper-prospect","t":"Sun on Prospect Street (Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"Cincinnati Art Museum"}]},{"n":8,"name":"Freight cars and the box factory","who":"<i>Freight Cars</i> and <i>Box Factory</i>, 1928","addr":"Maplewood Avenue and Cleveland Street","ll":[42.61943,-70.66763],"approx":false,"text":"The rail yard by the station gave Hopper <i>Freight Cars, Gloucester</i>, with the steeple of St. Ann’s Church behind the cars; the box factory on Cleveland Street has been torn down. The station is the end of the walk, with trains back to Boston.","works":[{"id":"hopper-freight-cars","t":"Freight Cars, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1928","img":false,"mus":"Addison Gallery of American Art, Andover"},{"id":"hopper-box-factory","t":"Box Factory, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1928","img":false,"mus":"The Museum of Modern Art, New York"}]},{"n":9,"name":"The Mansard Roof","who":"<i>The Mansard Roof</i>, 1923","addr":"2 Clarendon Street, Rocky Neck","ll":[42.60666,-70.65874],"approx":false,"text":"The Brooklyn Museum bought this watercolor after its 1923 show. The house was part of Augustus Wonson’s estate.","works":[{"id":"hopper-mansard","t":"The Mansard Roof","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1923","img":false,"mus":"Brooklyn Museum"}]},{"n":10,"name":"Above Cripple Cove","who":"<i>Gloucester Harbor</i>, 1912","addr":"East Main Street, East Gloucester","ll":[42.61505,-70.6484],"approx":true,"text":"Hopper’s 1912 oil of the harbor was painted from the ground above Cripple Cove. The marker is the cove; the exact spot is not recorded.","works":[{"id":"hopper-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":11,"name":"Briar Neck","who":"<i>Briar Neck, Gloucester</i>, 1912","addr":"Good Harbor Beach","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":true,"text":"The point at the end of Good Harbor Beach. The marker is the beach.","works":[{"id":"hopper-briar-neck","t":"Briar Neck, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":12,"name":"Squam Light","who":"<i>Houses of ’Squam Light</i>, 1923","addr":"Wigwam Point, Annisquam","ll":[42.6619,-70.68149],"approx":false,"text":"Hopper’s view of the light and its keeper’s houses. The light can be seen from Wingaersheek Beach across the river; the keeper’s house is Coast Guard housing (see site 8).","works":[{"id":"hopper-squam-light","t":"Houses of 'Squam Light, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1923","img":false,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}]}]},{"id":"rothko","url":"trails/rothko.html","title":"Rothko, Avery and Gottlieb","kicker":"East Gloucester","span":"1921 to 1945","short":"Half a day","lead":"In 1932 Milton Avery invited two young New York painters, Mark Rothko and Adolph Gottlieb, to Gloucester. They drew the bathers on the beaches and the wharfs across the harbor. Six stops, from the beach to the fish flakes.","legs":[[0,2,"drive"],[2,5,"walk"],[5,6,"drive"]],"hero":null,"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"Rothko’s wharf","addr":"27 Pleasant Street","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"In August 2026 Christopher Rothko gave the museum his father’s <i>Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)</i> of 1934, the first Rothko in its collection. It shows the salt-fish sheds of the Reed and Gamage wharfs near Smith Cove, painted from Banner Hill.","works":[{"id":"rothko-wharf-cam","t":"Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Mark Rothko","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"}]},{"n":2,"name":"Good Harbor Beach","who":"Bathers and handball","addr":"Thatcher Road","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":false,"text":"Avery painted the crowd watching the total solar eclipse of 1932 at Good Harbor. Two years later the friends met here daily: Edith Rothko remembered that they would “go swimming and play handball,” and Rothko painted the game.","works":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Avery’s studio","who":"Milton Avery (1885–1965)","addr":"77–78 Rocky Neck Avenue","ll":[42.60757,-70.6559],"approx":false,"text":"Avery first summered in Gloucester in 1921; in 1926 he and the sculptor Leonard Craske rented cottages here, the one record of where he stayed. His <i>Gloucester Dawn</i> of about 1921 is at Vassar.","works":[{"id":"avery-dawn","t":"Gloucester Dawn","by":"Milton Avery","d":"c. 1921","img":false,"mus":"Frances Lehman Loeb Art Center, Vassar College"}]},{"n":4,"name":"Smith Cove","who":"The wharfs","addr":"Rocky Neck causeway","ll":[42.60665,-70.65528],"approx":true,"text":"Smith Cove was the center of Gloucester’s gill-net fishing. Rothko painted its wharfs again and again in 1934: the Reed and Gamage salt-fish sheds with a water tank on the roof, and rows of fish flakes covered with white cloths to keep the sun off the fillets; fishermen among the gill-net reels on a wharf…","works":[{"id":"rothko-wharf-nga","t":"Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Mark Rothko","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"National Gallery of Art, Washington"}]},{"n":5,"name":"Banner Hill","who":"The high view","addr":"above 186–220 East Main Street","ll":[42.60644,-70.65084],"approx":true,"text":"The ridge above Smith Cove, where the Impressionists painted. Rothko painted the Reed and Gamage wharfs from Banner Hill, and Gottlieb sketched them from a similar spot, possibly beside him. The hilltop is private; look from the road.","works":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Calder Street","who":"Rothko, summer 1934","addr":"Calder Street, East Gloucester","ll":[42.60897,-70.64384],"approx":true,"text":"In the summer of 1934 Rothko and his wife, Edith Sachar, rented a house on Calder Street, about half a mile from the wharfs, while the Averys and the Gottliebs summered nearby. The Rothkos arrived first and took the house the Gottliebs had wanted. The house number is not recorded; this is a quiet residential street.","works":[]}]},{"id":"waterfront","url":"waterfront.html","title":"The working waterfront","kicker":"the harbor at work","span":"Fish flakes to draggers","short":"A harbor walk, then a short drive","lead":"The harbor in the paintings was a factory. This page explains what the painters were looking at — fish flakes, dories, schooners, nets — and where the working waterfront can still be seen.","legs":[[0,4,"walk"],[4,5,"drive"]],"hero":{"id":"homer-schooners-waterboat","alt":"Winslow Homer, Gloucester Schooners and Waterboat, 1880"},"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Fishermen’s Memorial","who":"","addr":"Stacy Boulevard at Babson Court","ll":[42.610051,-70.670223],"approx":false,"text":"Leonard Craske’s <i>Man at the Wheel</i>, dedicated August 23, 1925. The plaques list 5,368 men known to have been lost at sea; a single storm in 1862 took 120 men, and storms in 1879 took 159.","works":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Salting Fish marker","who":"","addr":"25 Rogers Street, at Porter Street","ll":[42.61171,-70.66452],"approx":false,"text":"At its peak Gorton’s ran dozens of wharves and many flake yards, where cod from the North Atlantic banks was split, salted and dried.","works":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Maritime Gloucester","who":"","addr":"Harbor Loop","ll":[42.61166,-70.66069],"approx":false,"text":"The harbor’s museum of its own working history, on the inner harbor.","works":[]},{"n":4,"name":"The Fort","who":"","addr":"Fort Square","ll":[42.60832,-70.66457],"approx":false,"text":"The point where Lane painted Ten Pound Island and Hopper painted the fishermen’s houses of the Italian Quarter.","works":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Smith Cove","who":"","addr":"East Gloucester","ll":[42.60665,-70.65528],"approx":false,"text":"Rothko’s 1934 wharfs had fish flakes on the pier, the last generation of the racks Lane had painted eighty years before.","works":[]}]}],"words":[{"t":"Fish flakes","d":"Low wooden racks on which split, salted cod was laid out in the sun to dry. Whole fields of them appear in Lane’s and later paintings of the waterfront.","w":null},{"t":"Gill nets","d":"Nets hung like a curtain in the water, catching fish by the gills. Mellen’s <i>Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)</i> shows crews setting them.","w":{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"about 1870","img":true,"mus":"Bowdoin College Museum of Art, Brunswick, Maine"}},{"t":"Dories","d":"Small flat-bottomed boats carried on the schooners and rowed out to set lines and nets; Gordon Parks photographed Gloucester crews laying nets by hand from them in 1943. Homer drew boys in dories in 1873.","w":{"id":"homer-boys-in-a-dory","t":"Boys in a Dory","by":"Winslow Homer","d":"1873","img":true,"mus":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York"}},{"t":"Schooners","d":"The fore-and-aft rigged vessels of the Gloucester fleet that brought fish home from the banks.","w":{"id":"homer-schooners-waterboat","t":"Gloucester Schooners and Waterboat","by":"Winslow Homer","d":"1880","img":true,"mus":"Philadelphia Museum of Art"}},{"t":"Draggers","d":"Motor trawlers that drag a net along the bottom; Emile Gruppé painted them at the wharves.","w":null},{"t":"Drydock","d":"A berth where a vessel is taken out of the water for repair. Hassam painted one in the 1890s.","w":{"id":"hassam-drydock","t":"Drydock, Gloucester","by":"Childe Hassam","d":"c. 1890s","img":true,"mus":"The Phillips Collection, Washington"}}],"chains":[{"title":"Lane’s circle","years":"1850s","steps":["William R. G. Mellen","took the Universalist pulpit, 1855","Mary Blood Mellen","worked beside him","Fitz Henry Lane"]},{"title":"Duveneck to Hopper","years":"1890–1923","steps":["Frank Duveneck","brought, 1890s","John H. Twachtman","taught","Leon Kroll","came with, 1912","Edward Hopper","courted here, 1923","Josephine Nivison"]},{"title":"The red cottage","years":"1914–1934","steps":["Charles Allan Winter","urged him to come, 1914","John Sloan","invited, 1915","Stuart Davis"]},{"title":"East Gloucester friends","years":"1919–1926","steps":["Ellen Day Hale","lent a Folly Cove cottage, early 1920s","Theresa Bernstein and William Meyerowitz","hosted","Milton Avery","next-door studio, 1924","Sally Michel"]},{"title":"Avery’s young friends","years":"1932–1945","steps":["Milton Avery","invited, 1932","Mark Rothko, Adolph Gottlieb and Barnett Newman","a studio, 1944; a shared summer, 1945","Aaron Siskind"]}]};
